        "text": "In this module, we'll discuss the different kinds of nodes blockchain network structure, and how conflicts are resolved in a permissionless setting. In blockchain systems, not all nodes are the same. Under the broadest definitions, any machine that interacts with the network in some way can be considered a node. So far, when we've talked about nodes, we've been discussing full nodes, nodes that have a full copy of all the blockchain's transactions. When a full node receives a block, it will perform checks on the contents of the block, including the timestamp, hash and diff difficulty, amongst other things. It must verify each of the transactions whether the inputs have previously been spent, whether the signature matches the public key, whether the sum of the inputs is greater than or equal to the sum of the outputs. If it's satisfied, the node will keep a copy of the block and propagate it to the rest of its peers.",
